From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/msm/dsi: phy: let the sm8350 MDSS parent own the iface clock

The DSI PHY adds its "iface" clock (disp_cc_mdss_ahb_clk) with pm_clk.
pm_clk_add() prepares a clock that has an enable op and keeps it prepared
until the device is removed; pm_clk_suspend() only disables it. A prepared
dispcc clock holds dispcc runtime-active through the CCF provider runtime PM,
so the dispcc power domain MMCX stays on at its enable corner, and the prepare
chain keeps bi_tcxo (the non-AO RPMh XO vote, which covers the sleep set)
prepared, all with the display off.

Evidence on r121 (kernel r36), screen off since Denial's DPMS off at 111 s,
read from per-clock debugfs files (clk_summary is not usable for this: it
runtime-resumes every clock provider, including the DSI PHY PLL and so its
MDSS parent, and shows the MDSS clocks enabled while it prints):
  disp_cc_mdss_ahb_clk  enable 0  prepare 1
  disp_cc_mdss_mdp_clk, gcc_disp_hf/sf_axi_clk, disp_cc_pll1  0/0
  ae00000.display-subsystem, ae94400.phy: suspended; mdss_gdsc off
  af00000.clock-controller (dispcc): active; genpd mmcx: on
The one remaining prepare is the PHY's pm_clk entry (PCE_STATUS_PREPARED).
A clk trace of the MDSS clocks shows mdss_runtime_resume/suspend enabling
and disabling them symmetrically; there is no MDSS enable leak.

The PHY is a child of the MDSS node, the MDSS lists "iface" and enables it in
mdss_runtime_resume(), and runtime PM keeps the parent active whenever the PHY
is (including when the CCF resumes the PHY for its PLL clock ops). So on
sm8350 the PHY's own reference is redundant. Skip the pm_clk entry there; the
runtime PM ops then have no clock list and only propagate to the parent.

Replacing pm_clk with clk_prepare_enable()/clk_disable_unprepare() in the
PHY's runtime PM callbacks would release the prepare too, but unprepare takes
the CCF prepare_lock from the runtime-PM worker while a prepare_lock holder
can be waiting in rpm_resume() for that same PHY: an ABBA deadlock.

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/phy/dsi_phy.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/phy/dsi_phy.c
@@ -610,6 +610,14 @@
 	return -EINVAL;
 }
 
+static bool dsi_phy_parent_provides_iface(struct device *dev)
+{
+	struct device_node *np = dev->parent ? dev->parent->of_node : NULL;
+
+	return np && of_device_is_compatible(np, "qcom,sm8350-mdss") &&
+	       of_property_match_string(np, "clock-names", "iface") >= 0;
+}
+
 static int dsi_phy_driver_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 {
 	struct msm_dsi_phy *phy;
@@ -687,13 +695,26 @@
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
-	ret = devm_pm_clk_create(dev);
-	if (ret)
-		return ret;
+	/*
+	 * pm_clk keeps a clock that has an enable op prepared from pm_clk_add()
+	 * until the device goes away; runtime suspend only disables it. For the
+	 * dispcc "iface" (MDSS AHB) clock that prepare holds dispcc
+	 * runtime-active (so MMCX stays voted, in the RPMh sleep set too) and
+	 * keeps bi_tcxo, the RPMh XO vote, prepared with the display off.
+	 *
+	 * When the parent MDSS lists "iface" it already enables that clock in
+	 * its runtime resume, and runtime PM keeps the parent active whenever
+	 * this PHY is, so the PHY does not need its own reference.
+	 */
+	if (!dsi_phy_parent_provides_iface(dev)) {
+		ret = devm_pm_clk_create(dev);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
 
-	ret = pm_clk_add(dev, "iface");
-	if (ret < 0)
-		return dev_err_probe(dev, ret, "Unable to get iface clk\n");
+		ret = pm_clk_add(dev, "iface");
+		if (ret < 0)
+			return dev_err_probe(dev, ret, "Unable to get iface clk\n");
+	}
 
 	if (phy->cfg->ops.pll_init) {
 		ret = phy->cfg->ops.pll_init(phy);
